You ask someone to count the names on a sign-up sheet. Halfway through
they say, ``One page is missing,'' then carry on counting. You would not
write ``One page is missing'' into the list of names. On a terminal,
though, the count and such a warning can appear in the same place.
They can still travel by different channels. Once you know which
channel carries what, you can keep a warning visible without treating
it as part of your data.

\begin{objectives}
\item Identify standard input, standard output, and standard error even when they use the same terminal.
\item Tell when a command reads the terminal and finish an interactive input with end-of-file.
\item Distinguish a command's output, its diagnostic messages, and its exit status.
\end{objectives}

\section{Read a file, then read the keyboard}
\label{sec:15-read}
\IndexCommand{cat}
\IndexCommand{wc}
\IndexTerm{standard input}
Start a practice \cmd|sh| if necessary; leave it with \cmd|exit|
after the exercises. Make a new directory. \cmd|mktemp -d| creates
it; \cmd|$(...)| captures the name for \cmd|lab| and \cmd|&&| makes
each subsequent command depend on the success of the previous one.
Stop if the new path isn't printed. Record it for cleanup.

\begin{terminal}
lab=$(mktemp -d) && cd "$lab" && pwd
printf '%s\n' Asha Bea Chen > roll.txt
\end{terminal}

Only run the second line after you have confirmed the first one.
\cmd|printf| prints each name followed by a newline; the shell's
\cmd|>| sends that text to the named file rather than to the screen
and would overwrite an existing file. Here the directory is new.
You can display the text or count its lines:

\begin{terminal}
cat roll.txt
wc -l roll.txt
\end{terminal}

\cmd|cat| copies the file's contents to its output in order. The
\cmd|wc| command counts things in its input; \cmd|-l| selects
newline characters. In this fixture every name ends with a newline,
so the answer is three. The count may be padded with spaces and is
followed by the filename because you supplied it as an operand.
Neither command opens a special window to show the result. Both
write into an output channel that is normally connected to the
terminal during an interactive session.

\begin{terminal}
wc -l
\end{terminal}

This version of the command names no file. Ordinarily it reads
from \emph{standard input}, its regular incoming channel, which
for this command at an interactive prompt is usually connected
to the terminal. The absence of a new shell prompt means the
program still has your attention. Type \texttt{Asha}, press
\EnterKey, then type \texttt{Bea} and press \EnterKey.
At the start of the otherwise empty next line press
\KeyChord{Ctrl}{D}. Expect the number two without a filename, then
the shell's prompt. The names you saw while typing were generally
echoed by the terminal settings; they weren't an early report from
\cmd|wc|. Reports that count an entire input have to wait until
the input ends.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering\input{figures/chapter-15/channels.tikz}
\caption{A keyboard and a screen can serve three different channels.}
\label{fig:15-channels}
\VisualDescription{Standard input leads into the command from the terminal. Standard output and standard error leave through separate channels, both leading to the terminal display in this example.}
\end{figure}

In that example a file operand meant that \cmd|wc| opened the
named file itself. Leaving the operand out meant that it used a
connection the shell passed to it. Both ways can deliver the same
bytes, but they are not the same mechanism. Later you will connect
that incoming channel to a file or another command. Learning
that distinction now prevents a common confusion: when a command
has an input file operand, changing its standard input may have
no effect at all on the data it reads.

\begin{quicktable}{M{1.4in}Y}
\toprule
Channel & During a typical terminal session \\
\midrule
0: standard input & A program that reads this channel can use your terminal input; if it reads a named file instead, this channel may sit unused. \\
1: standard output & Expected results, for example the line count from wc, normally appear on your terminal. \\
2: standard error & Diagnostics and sometimes progress or prompts normally appear on the same terminal, but arrive through a separate connection. \\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

\section{Know when you have finished typing}
\label{sec:15-eof}
\IndexTerm{end-of-file}
With normal terminal settings, \KeyChord{Ctrl}{D} on an empty
line lets a program reading from the terminal see that there is
no more input at that read. This is an \emph{end-of-file}
condition, often shortened to EOF. It is not the text
\texttt{EOF} and not a newline. With pending text on a line,
the same key can instead make those pending characters available
without a newline. For this chapter always press \EnterKey\ after
the text and use \KeyChord{Ctrl}{D} on the empty line that follows.
Terminal controls can be changed; the instructions assume the usual
interactive line settings.

\begin{terminal}
cat
\end{terminal}

Type \texttt{blue table}, then \EnterKey. You are likely to see
this phrase twice: once as the terminal shows what you typed
and once as \cmd|cat| writes it back through its output channel.
Finish on an empty line with \KeyChord{Ctrl}{D}. Don't type another
shell command before the prompt returns. If you do, it may go to
\cmd|cat| as data, not to the shell as a command. In an ordinary
interactive session, \KeyChord{Ctrl}{C} interrupts a foreground
command instead; if a practice command is stuck, that is a way
to stop it rather than claiming you finished supplying input.
An EOF lets the command finish its job; an interrupt is a
request to stop it.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering\input{figures/chapter-15/eof.tikz}
\caption{A newline completes a line; end-of-file lets the count finish.}
\label{fig:15-eof}
\VisualDescription{Two complete name lines enter the counter. End-of-file on the following empty line, not the second newline, releases the total.}
\end{figure}

\section{A screen is not a channel label}
\label{sec:15-diagnostics}
\IndexTerm{standard output}
\IndexTerm{standard error}
\IndexTerm{file descriptor}
A running program can have several open connections to files,
devices, or other endpoints at the same time. A \emph{file
descriptor} is the number it uses to refer to one of those open
connections. By convention, descriptor 0 means standard input,
1 means standard output, and 2 means standard error. The numbers
are not permanent addresses for a particular keyboard, screen,
or file on disk. They describe where to read or write in this
running program. A shell can rearrange what those descriptors
are connected to when it launches a command.

\begin{terminal}
cat roll.txt not-here.txt
printf 'Status: %s\n' "$?"
\end{terminal}

The two operands prompt two file reads. Find the three names,
a complaint about the file that wasn't created, and a nonzero
status printed by the separate \cmd|printf| call. The exact
diagnostic wording varies. The three names are ordinary output;
the complaint is on standard error. The status came back to
the shell, which then handed it to \cmd|printf| as data.
It was not a line \cmd|cat| printed. The example also shows
why partial results and failure can coexist: a command can
succeed in reading the first file and still fail to finish
the overall request.

\begin{terminal}
cat roll.txt not-here.txt > visible.txt
printf 'Status: %s\n' "$?"
cat visible.txt
\end{terminal}

In this fresh directory the first call creates \texttt{visible.txt}
from descriptor 1 without moving descriptor 2. The diagnostic
still appears on the terminal. Reading the new file shows only
the three names; \cmd|cat| still returned nonzero for the
compound file request. The filename \texttt{visible.txt} is
intentionally ironic: the normal output doesn't become visible
on your screen until you read the file. Chapter~\ref{ch:16}
covers why the file may appear even when a command cannot
produce any output at all.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering\input{figures/chapter-15/separate.tikz}
\caption{When results go to a file, the warning can stay on the terminal.}
\label{fig:15-separate}
\VisualDescription{The command sends the valid name lines through descriptor 1 to the output file and the missing-file diagnostic through descriptor 2 to the terminal. A separate completion status returns to the shell.}
\end{figure}

\begin{quicktable}{M{1.35in}Y}
\toprule
What you notice & One useful check \\
\midrule
No prompt, no output & Is the command reading from the terminal? Was a file operand intended? \\
Output and complaint & Check whether the data is complete; look up the meaning of the exit status. \\
Output file but no screen result & Read the file if you redirected output; check the status before trusting the file. \\
Screen text while typing & Ask whether the terminal itself echoed input instead of the command printing a result. \\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

\section{Name a file or supply the channel}
\label{sec:15-file-or-channel}
The \cmd|-| operand tells \cmd|cat| to read standard input at
that point in its list of inputs. You can use it to place a
short handwritten line before the names from the file:

\begin{terminal}
cat - roll.txt
\end{terminal}

Type \texttt{Saturday names}, press \EnterKey, then press
\KeyChord{Ctrl}{D} at the start of the next line. Expect your
own input back as data and then the three names from the file.
\cmd|cat| processes its input operands in order. It doesn't
know whether the first input is a heading and doesn't change
letter case or add spacing. A real file named exactly \texttt{-}
would need to be written \cmd|./-| here, because this utility
treats the bare operand specially (see Chapter~\ref{ch:13}).

\begin{terminal}
wc -l < roll.txt
\end{terminal}

\cmd|<| asks the shell to connect standard input to the file.
There is now no named file operand for \cmd|wc| to print
alongside the count. Expect three and the prompt, without
having to type \KeyChord{Ctrl}{D}: the file's end supplies
EOF. If two commands both accept standard input, a file
prepared for one can sometimes be given to the other without
creating an intermediate file in a special format. That is
useful only if both agree on how to interpret the bytes.

\begin{dialoguebox}{Same screen, different channels}
\Brian{A result and a complaint appeared on the same screen. How can they be separate?}
\Stephen{The program wrote them on two channels that happen to reach your terminal.}
\Brian{Then why not call everything on the screen output?}
\Stephen{You can casually, but when moving the results you need to know which channel you're moving.}
\Brian{So if I save the results, the complaint can stay on my screen?}
\Stephen{Yes, and the saved results can still be incomplete.}
\Brian{The data and the complaint tell me different things. What tells the shell I should check?}
\Stephen{The exit status. It is a separate report, not another line of text.}
\DialogueConclusion{One screen can carry distinct channels.}
\end{dialoguebox}

\section{Work out who has the keyboard}
\label{sec:15-waiting}
If a program seems stuck, look at the recent history of
the session before repeating the command. Did you enter
a command that can read standard input, without naming
a file? If so, a quiet terminal can mean the program is
ready for data, not that the machine is frozen. If the
shell's ordinary prompt is present instead, the shell
probably has control. Don't press \KeyChord{Ctrl}{D} at
the shell's prompt just to see what will happen: in that
context it can end the session. Which program is reading
matters as much as which keys you use.

Try a deliberate interruption in the lab. Run \cmd|wc -l|,
type one name followed by \EnterKey, and on the next line
press \KeyChord{Ctrl}{C} instead of \KeyChord{Ctrl}{D}.
With ordinary settings you get the prompt back without a
count. The printed notation, such as \texttt{\^{}C}, depends
on the terminal. Now start the same command again, type the
same line, and finish with EOF. Expect one. Terminating a job
doesn't give it a chance to finish its report in the same
way that ending input does.

A different reason for waiting is that a program has already
read some data and needs more. A count of two names in a
typed session looks immediate after EOF, but the counter
can't say two before it knows there won't be a third.
Contrast that with \cmd|cat|: it can copy some data before
the entire input is available. This difference belongs to
what the command does, not to standard input itself. Both
used descriptor 0. A bigger task could be quiet for a long
time while reading valid input, or could show some results
and still be waiting. Don't send an interrupt merely because
one program doesn't show progress the way another does.

\section{Make an empty input on purpose}
\label{sec:15-empty}
An intentional empty input is different from an attempt to
read a file that isn't there. All the systems discussed
here provide \cmd|/dev/null|: as an input, it supplies
immediate EOF, and as an output it discards the data sent
to it. Only read from it in this example. The slash at
the start makes the device path absolute, so your working
directory doesn't affect where the read goes.

\begin{terminal}
wc -l < /dev/null
printf 'Count status: %s\n' "$?"
wc -l roll.txt < /dev/null
\end{terminal}

The first count is zero, with status zero. The second count
is three with the filename. Connecting something to
descriptor 0 doesn't obligate the utility to consume it.
Its operand names the file it is to count. Reaching the
end of a valid, perhaps empty, input is not an error.
The earlier operation with the missing file couldn't open
its input, so it gave a diagnostic instead. A zero can tell
you an input was empty only if you know you received the
data you intended to count.

In a real terminal session you might want to send someone
the result of a command and separately tell them it didn't
process one input. Separate those claims even when writing
by hand. Record what output you observed, where it was
written, and whether the status signaled an issue. A note
that says ``nothing printed'' does not settle whether a
command was reading, finished, or redirected. State what
you supplied and what happened to the prompt. This makes
it easier to explain the problem without dumping an entire,
possibly private, session transcript.

\section{Compare typed input with an exact file copy}
The way EOF arrives can change without changing the data
the consumer receives. To see that, read the same two
lines once from your keyboard and once from a file. Start
the counter as before. Enter \texttt{Asha}, \EnterKey,
\texttt{Bea}, \EnterKey, and \KeyChord{Ctrl}{D}
on the empty line. Note the count. Now create the new
fixture \texttt{two.txt} in the lab only, then supply it
on the same input channel.

\begin{terminal}
printf '%s\n' Asha Bea > two.txt
wc -l < two.txt
\end{terminal}

Both counts are two. During the second run the input
lines aren't echoed, because the terminal isn't the source.
The counter still sees two newline characters followed by
EOF. The example would give a different result if you
pressed \KeyChord{Ctrl}{D} before pressing \EnterKey\ on
the final typed name: even if that name reaches the program,
it doesn't contribute a newline to the count. Also do not
confuse echo with a saved record. Typing directly into the
counter doesn't preserve a copy for you to review. The text
file can be reviewed and reused with another utility.

When a tool asks an interactive question it may use a
channel differently from a plain counter. A prompt can
appear on standard error so it isn't mixed into normal
output. Some programs deliberately open the terminal for
conversation even when their ordinary input is attached
to a file. The three standard descriptors are conventions
about connections, not a rule forbidding a program from
making others. Before sending data to an unfamiliar tool,
find out where it expects input and whether it is meant
to talk to a person. If you don't see a new shell prompt
afterward, read what the program is asking rather than
blindly typing a second command.

For paper-like forms such as this roll, choosing the file
as input means the data doesn't depend on keystrokes during
the count. If you need to repeat the count, the same file
can supply the same lines again. A manual test is valuable
for learning what a stream is, but a preserved fixture
makes it easier to decide whether a change in the answer
came from a change in the input.

\section{Practice and cleanup}
\begin{exercise}{Count a typed attendance sheet}
Run a newline count using the terminal as input. Type four short
names on separate lines. Finish the stream, then decide which
visible text is typed input and which is the program's answer.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
Run \cmd|wc -l| without a filename. Press \EnterKey\ after
each name, then \KeyChord{Ctrl}{D} on the empty line. The
program reports four without a filename. The terminal normally
echoes the names before that report. This writes no file.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Explain a mixed result}
Read the existing name list and a nonexistent file in a single
command, keeping standard output on the terminal. Check the
status immediately and identify which messages are normal data.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
Use \cmd|cat roll.txt not-here.txt|, then
\cmd|printf '%s\n' "$?"|. The three name lines are normal
output and the missing-file message is a diagnostic.
The printed status is nonzero. This exercise leaves no files.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Count without a printed filename}
Count the file's lines without typing the names and without
giving the counter a file operand. Predict how the report
differs from the original file-operand example.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
Use \cmd|wc -l < roll.txt|. Expect three without
\texttt{roll.txt} alongside it. The incoming connection
does the work, and no cleanup is needed for the count.
\end{solution}

Check \cmd|pwd| against the path you recorded before removing
anything. The following removes the practice files with confirmation;
omit \texttt{visible.txt} or \texttt{two.txt} if you didn't create
them. The nonrecursive \cmd|rmdir|
will refuse to delete a directory that still holds files;
inspect it instead of forcing removal.

\begin{terminal}
rm -i roll.txt visible.txt two.txt
cd ..
rmdir "$lab"
\end{terminal}

\begin{takeaways}
\item Standard input, output, and error are numbered connections, not fixed physical devices.
\item End-of-file ends a read; an interrupt stops an activity rather than finishing its input.
\item Data, diagnostics, and completion status are separate evidence about a command.
\end{takeaways}

